WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.600
Hi, I'm Ethan Yang. I'm a junior at Fayston Preparatory School in Seoul.

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:08.800
For the past two years, I've been researching jellyfish blooms,

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.600
especially the damage they cause to fisheries and coastal infrastructure across Korea, China and Japan.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:19.600
Last year, I published a research paper on converting jellyfish biomass into agricultural fertilizer.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:25.600
This year, I placed in the top 500 at the Blue Ocean Competition with a business model built around the same idea.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.600
The gap I kept running into was data.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:31.600
Korea, China and Japan each monitor jellyfish separately.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:36.600
There's no shared database that crosses borders.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:39.600
JellyWatch is my first attempt to build one,

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:45.600
using citizen volunteers as the monitoring network. If you live near the coast in any of the three countries, I'd like your help.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:50.600
You can report a sighting, request a free monitoring kit, or just follow the data on the map.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:55.600
The link is on the site. Hope to hear from many fellow citizen scientists. Thank you.
